{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>And they said: Draw near, pray.</b> קְרַב לְהָלְאָה — that is, move close to the sides and move away from him. And so every הָלְאָה in Scripture is a term of distance, as in ״זְרֵה הָלְאָה״ (Numbers 17), ״הִנֵּה הַחֵצִי מִמְּךָ וָהָלְאָה״ (I Samuel 20), ״גֵּשׁ הָלְאָה״ (Isaiah 49) — pull yourself away over there, in the language of la'az טריטי״דנוש. And it is a word of rebuke, saying: we are not afraid of you. And similar to it: ״קְרַב אֵלֶיךָ אַל תִּגַּשׁ בִּי״ (Isaiah 65), and likewise ״גְּשָׁה לִּי וְאֵשֵׁבָה״ (ibid. 49) — move to the sides for my sake and I will sit beside you. You intercede on behalf of the guests — how does your heart move you? Because he said to them concerning the daughters, they said to him <b>draw near, pray</b> — a term of enticement. And because he was interceding on behalf of the guests — <b>This one fellow came in to sojourn</b>: a lone foreigner you are among us, who came to sojourn — <b>and will he judge?</b>: and you make yourself our reprover?" "<b>The door.</b> It is the surrounding frame, for locking and unlocking."], :book :genesis, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {0 "Rashi reads the men of Sodom's words in two stages: an initial enticement (גש הלאה, addressed when Lot mentioned his daughters) and a sharp rebuke about his defense of the guests. The la'az טריטי״דנוש reflects Old French 'tret'enous' or similar, meaning 'move yourself away.'"}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:59:29.436433030Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}